# Target C: seven-no-root `D=-2, k=3` closure

Date: 2026-09-20

Status: exact closure of the smallest remaining seven-no-root rank-density branch. This does not close the whole seven-no-root layer and does not change the direct 3,874-cell generic-H6 endpoint.

Companion verifier:

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k3_dminus2_2026_09_20.py`

## Starting point

The preceding seven-no-root depth-two deletion theorem proves that any hypothetical seven-no-root counterexample `B` has

`D(B) in {-2,-1}`

and satisfies the exact identity

`D(B) = 7 + p + 3d - 3k`.

In the `D=-2, k=3` branch this forces

`d=0, p=0`.

The global coefficient-constraint space `P_7` therefore has rank

`7-k = 4`.

The same deletion theorem gives more structure. Every one-row deletion keeps the quotient constraint rank equal to four, and every two-row deletion also keeps it equal to four. Equivalently, projection of `P_7` after deleting any one or any two coordinates is injective on the row space. In coding language,

`minimum support(P_7) >= 3`.

Every two-row deletion is a five-no-root shadow with

`D=2, k=1, d=0, p=0`,

and every one-row deletion is a six-no-root shadow with

`D=0, k=2, d=0, p=0`.

This makes a deletion-shadow enumeration much smaller than a scan of all 4,546,114 seven-subset `S_7` orbits.

## Exact five-row boundary census

For each of the 20,451 `S_7` orbit representatives of five no-root four-cycles, the verifier enumerates every rank-four coefficient space generated by empty root-block constraints. For a fixed coefficient space, a touched block is canonically empty when its empty-block constraint is contained in `P` and canonically complete otherwise. Only states whose mandatory complete-block relation set has deficiency zero are retained.

The exhaustive scan gives exactly

- 340 five-row coefficient states;
- on 74 of the 20,451 five-cycle orbit representatives.

The scan was run in four disjoint orbit ranges. Their state counts were

```text
0..5000        204
5000..10000      4
10000..15000   100
15000..20451    32
------------------
total           340
```

The orbit representative list used in that run was checked to be exactly the list produced by the committed `five_no_root_orbits()` routine.

This canonical enumeration is sufficient for an actual `d=0,p=0` state. Completing an additional compatible empty block does not change the coefficient space, while the mandatory canonical complete blocks form a subset of the actual complete-block relation set. If the actual complete-block set is independent, so is that mandatory subset.

## Exact six-row synchronized shadow census

Each of the 340 five-row states was extended by each of the 100 remaining no-root cycles, giving 34,000 parent-plus-cycle extensions.

For each extension the verifier enumerates every obtainable rank-four `P_6` whose projection to the chosen five coordinates is the parent `P_5`. It then imposes the condition required by a seven-row `D=-2` parent: deleting any of the six coordinates must still leave rank four. It also requires canonical complete-block deficiency zero.

The result is

```text
five-row parent states             340
sixth-cycle extensions          34,000
raw synchronized states             63
normalized labeled states            47
six-cycle sets                         3
```

The 47 states have complete-block census

```text
5 complete blocks    15 states
6 complete blocks    32 states
```

The three six-cycle sets have a simple support description. Each is the union of all three four-cycles on one four-vertex set and all three four-cycles on a second four-vertex set. The two four-vertex sets meet in respectively three, two, or one vertices.

This six-row stage was rerun with the committed verifier logic and reproduced all counts above.

## Seventh-cycle extension and depth-two failure

Every one of the 47 six-row states was extended by each of the 99 remaining no-root cycles. Thus the final extension cover has

`47 * 99 = 4,653`

cases.

For every case, the empty-block constraints admit exactly one obtainable rank-four `P_7` that projects to the given `P_6` and still has rank four after every single-coordinate deletion.

None survives the required pair-deletion test.

The exact census is

```text
seventh-cycle extensions                    4,653
single-deletion-synchronized P_7            4,653
minimum pair-projection rank = 3            4,653
minimum pair-projection rank = 4                0
```

The number of bad coordinate pairs per extension is

```text
1 bad pair       36
2 bad pairs      39
3 bad pairs      24
4 bad pairs   1,212
6 bad pairs   3,342
```

In every case the bad pair lies entirely among the six parent coordinates; none of the 4,653 cases requires the newly added seventh coordinate to witness the failure.

Thus every obtainable single-deletion-synchronized rank-four extension contains a nonzero coefficient constraint supported on at most two coordinates. This contradicts the depth-two theorem.

Therefore

> there is no seven-no-root counterexample with `D=-2` and `k=3`.

Since the defect identity forces `d=p=0` in this branch, the entire `D=-2,k=3` branch is closed.

## Exactness

Coefficient-space generation, union, projection, and containment are exact rational row-space operations inherited from the committed local-block verifier.

The only complete-block rank decisions in this computation use the existing `2^127-1` modular rank implementation. The base verifier checks primality by Lucas-Lehmer and the Hadamard bound for the complete-block relation rows. The present computation introduces no new `T`-space relation-row type, so that exactness certificate applies unchanged.

Validation of the committed implementation included:

- Python bytecode compilation;
- an independent five-orbit shard run (`0..1000`) giving 61 states on 10 orbit representatives;
- a full six-row extension rerun reproducing 34,000 extensions, 63 raw states, and 47 normalized states;
- a full seven-row extension rerun reproducing all 4,653 pair-rank failures and zero survivors.

## Remaining seven-no-root boundary

The `D=-2,k=3` point is gone. The smallest remaining seven-no-root cases are now

```text
D=-2, k=4: (d,p) = (0,3) or (1,0)
D=-1, k=3: (d,p) = (0,1)
```

The deletion-shadow route should next classify the six-row boundary shadows needed by one of those cases rather than enumerate the full seven-subset orbit layer.

The direct C-II endpoint remains unchanged:

`3874 = 3076 C7 + 798 (C3 disjoint-union C4)`.

No theorem-state authority file is changed by this result.
